{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from Rephidim.</b> Their journeying from Rephidim was in repentance, but before their journeying from Rephidim they were not in repentance — to fulfill the words of our Sages that it was called Rephidim because they slackened their hands from the Torah." "<b>And they encamped in the wilderness.</b> A public domain — whoever wishes to receive it may come and receive it." "<b>Opposite the mountain.</b> Rashi: to its east. It is not stated in Rashi 'to its east,' for if so it would imply that they crossed the mountain and encamped to its east, and how is it possible to say that they crossed Mount Sinai before the giving of the Torah? Rather, Rashi [says] to its east, and this is what it says: <b>And Israel encamped there opposite</b> the east of Mount Sinai — for ever since they journeyed from them, their faces were always toward the east until they came to the land of Moab, as Rashi explained in the section of Masei. And this is what Rashi explains here: wherever you find 'opposite,' it means facing east, and thus it is in the Mekhilta: 'opposite the mountain — to the east of the mountain.' And regarding what Rashi explained that 'opposite' means 'facing' — but is it not written מנגד, 'opposite, all around the Tent of Meeting they shall encamp'? [This is resolved as follows:]"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:58:41.779872949Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}